<<~ ahu #/identity-stack>>

! Identity Stack — DID / Handle / lar: Alias

> ''PRE-KEYHIVE MODEL — superseded, rewrite deferred (operator, 2026-06-24).'' This stack predates the
> ruled identity model. The live model is ''capability-is-identity + Keyhive concap'' (the capability IS
> the identity; petnames over the lar: l-space regions) — <<~ loulou lar:///ha.ka.ba/lares/api/pono/lararium-identity>>.
> What this file describes (Elyncia.app / DreamDeck on AT Protocol `did:plc` + UCAN) reads as a ''web2-bridge''
''> app layer'' behind a causal-island boundary (Web3-only law), NOT the Lares-stack-proper identity. Held for a
> later rewrite pass.

The Elyncia.app / DreamDeck identity model holds three distinct layers. ''Do not conflate them.''

| Layer | Form | What it holds |
|---|---|---|
| ''DID'' | `did:plc:abc123` | AT Protocol canonical identity — the cryptographic key holder. Resolved via Bluesky auth (OAuth over DID). This names the actual principal in UCAN capability tokens. |
| ''Handle'' | `@telarus.elyncia.social` (AT Protocol/Bluesky) or `@telarus@elyncia.social` (ActivityPub/Kowloon) | A resolution alias over the DID — human-readable, not authoritative. |
| ''lar: alias'' | `telarus:operator@enyalios` | Application-layer signal state — names the //operational role// of the speaker in a `lar:` exchange. Not a network identity; not a DID alias. |

''Why the lar: alias carries no leading `@`:'' the handle (`@telarus@elyncia.social`) already serves as a resolution alias over the DID. The lar: `alias` field holds a third distinct thing — it tags the operational role in the signal exchange. Adding `@` to a lar: alias would conflate the social-handle layer with the application-signal layer.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/handle-form>>

!! Handle Form

Within the DreamDeck / Kowloon ActivityPub layer, identities use the canonical ActivityPub two-part handle structure:

```
@alias@node
```

| ActivityPub handle | lar: URI authority | Underlying DID |
|---|---|---|
| `@lindwyrm@new-delos` | `lindwyrm:...@new-delos` | `did:plc:...` (Lindwyrm's key) |
| `@telarus@~crossroads` | `telarus:operator@enyalios` | `did:plc:...` (Telarus's key) |
| `@mischief-muse@lares` | `mischief-muse:agent@lares-abc123` | Lares node DID or ephemeral key |

The `~crossroads` tilde prefix denotes a nomadic/crossroads node — no fixed host, routes through the nearest stable nexus.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/post-header-render>>

!! DreamDeck Post-Header Render Target

The `chat-log:post-header` render target carries the in-chat-log, timestamped projection for post headers.

''DreamDeck post header format (canonical):''
```
@handle@node — timestamp — //ha.ka.ba/lares/{optional/path/} [confidence] 🏛️{tc}🌊{tc}🗡️{tc}🎭{tc}🔮{tc}
```

The attitude triple (`//ha.ka.ba`) sits ''before'' other instruments like confidence and stance — grounds bearing before posture (BEARING → HOW, matching the URI path-first layout logic governed by lar-uri).

| Render target | Surface | URIs canonical? | When used |
|---|---|---|---|
| `chat-log:post-header` | `@handle@node — timestamp — //ha.ka.ba{/path} [Reg] 🏛️{tc}…` | No — social projection with glyphs | DreamDeck feed posts, BBS thread headers |
| `hud:exchange-pair` | `<<~ lares aim …>>` + `hud`·`ward` panel beneath | ''Yes — canonical ASCII URI''; only the sigil panel uses glyphs | Every exchange-span boundary (mandatory) |
| `record:full` | `lar://alias:grant@host/ha.ka.ba/lares/` | Yes — identity projection | Storage, crystal serialization, registry |

The `record:full` and `hud:exchange-pair` render targets live in the lar-uri scheme itself; `chat-log:post-header` rides here because it projects identity + bearing onto the social surface.

<<~/ahu>>

<<~ ahu #/stance-carry>>

!! Stance Tool-Carry in Render Targets

In HUD render targets, stance tool-carry modifiers attach directly to the preceding stance emoji as a two-character pair (no space) — `🏛️/*!`. The tool grammar itself (Wand · Cup · Sword · Pentacle · Stone) lives in the four-tools law; this surface only fixes the //attachment// convention for render targets. The `syad` lens carries the invoked standpoints; the optional `:` binds a tool to a standpoint.

See `the-four-tools` and `the-syad-perspectives` for the tool and standpoint grammar.

<<~/ahu>>

